internal/llm/fallback_test.go  # deterministic regression test (+ guards)
```

## 4. Verification

Before the fix:
```console
$ go test ./internal/llm/ -run TestBudget_CallerDeadlineIsNotRetriedAcrossTheChain -v -count=1
=== RUN   TestBudget_CallerDeadlineIsNotRetriedAcrossTheChain
    fallback_test.go:41: HTTP requests = 0, want 1: an expired caller deadline must not spend the rest of the chain
--- FAIL: TestBudget_CallerDeadlineIsNotRetriedAcrossTheChain (0.00s)
FAIL
```

After the fix:
```console
$ gofmt -l . && go vet ./...
$ go test ./internal/llm/ -run TestBudget_CallerDeadlineIsNotRetriedAcrossTheChain -v -count=1
=== RUN   TestBudget_CallerDeadlineIsNotRetriedAcrossTheChain
--- PASS: TestBudget_CallerDeadlineIsNotRetriedAcrossTheChain (0.00s)
PASS
ok  	trouble/internal/llm	0.004s
```

Full suite with race detector, repeated:
```console
$ go test ./... -race -count=20
ok  	trouble/internal/llm	4.075s
```

Guard tests included:
- `TestBudget_AttemptTimeoutStillFailsOver` — an attempt-local timeout (`ReasonAttemptTimeout`) still advances to the next candidate, confirming the deadline break didn't disable ordinary failover (`requests == 2`).
- `TestBudget_HappyPathSingleRequest` — happy path still issues exactly one request.

## 5. Takeaways

- **Enforce deadline semantics at the loop level.** `Retryable(ClassTimeout) == true` is fine for attempt-local timeouts, but a caller deadline is terminal and must `break`, not `continue`.
- **Never gate a wire attempt on a pre-wire `ctx.Err()` check.** It turns slow setup into "zero requests" and hides on warm machines. Issue on `context.WithoutCancel(ctx)` bounded by the attempt cap, then re-check the caller deadline after `Do` and after body read.
- **Make expiry tests deterministic** by cancelling at loop entry instead of relying on a machine-speed race, and assert the wire count (`requests == 1`), not just the returned error.
